{
  "module": "Output side: from scores to text",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "logits-softmax",
    "title": "Logits and softmax",
    "summary": "How the model's raw output scores (logits) are turned into probabilities: what logits are, why they are not probabilities, and how softmax converts them.",
    "covers": ["logits", "softmax"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "tokens",
        "reason": "There is one logit per token in the vocabulary, so the vocabulary must already be clear."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "scores",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "When the network has finished working on a piece of text, it has to say which token is likely to come next. It does that with "
            },
            {
              "text": "one score for every token in the vocabulary",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "These raw output scores are called "
            },
            {
              "text": "logits",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". A higher logit means that token is more likely to come next."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A vocabulary of V tokens means V logits: Llama 2 has 32,000 of them at each position, GPT-2 has 50,257."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 2, 13)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "not-probabilities",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Logits are "
            },
            {
              "text": "not probabilities",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". They can be negative, they can be larger than 1, and they do not add up to 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Here is a toy example with a vocabulary of only four tokens. The invented logits are 3, 2, 1, -1 for the tokens "
            },
            {
              "text": "mat",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", "
            },
            {
              "text": "sofa",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", "
            },
            {
              "text": "floor",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", "
            },
            {
              "text": "moon",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "You can see which token the model prefers, but you cannot read them as chances. A score of 3 does not mean a 300% chance."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 13)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "distribution",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What we want is a "
            },
            {
              "text": "probability distribution",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": one number for each token, where every number is between 0 and 1 and all the numbers add up to exactly 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "That is something you can sample from, and something that says how likely each token is. So we need a step that turns any list of scores into such a distribution, keeping larger scores as larger probabilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 14)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "exp-step",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The first half of the trick is the exponential function, "
            },
            {
              "text": "exp",
              "code": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". Whatever you put in, negative or positive, it returns a number that is "
            },
            {
              "text": "always positive",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", and a bigger input gives a bigger output."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "exp(3) is about 20.09"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "exp(2) is about 7.39"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "exp(1) is about 2.72"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "exp(-1) is about 0.37"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "So applying "
            },
            {
              "text": "exp",
              "code": true
            },
            {
              "text": " to the logits removes the negative numbers and keeps the order. The second half is to make the numbers add up to 1: divide each one by the total of all of them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 14)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "softmax-formula",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Putting both halves together gives "
            },
            {
              "text": "softmax",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". For a list of scores z, the probability of entry i is:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "\\mathrm{softmax}(z)_i = \\frac{e^{z_i}}{\\sum_j e^{z_j}}",
          "spoken": "softmax of z, at position i, equals e to the power z i, divided by the sum over j of e to the power z j"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The top is "
            },
            {
              "text": "exp",
              "code": true
            },
            {
              "text": " of that token's score. The bottom is the total of "
            },
            {
              "text": "exp",
              "code": true
            },
            {
              "text": " over all tokens, which is what makes everything add up to 1."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 14)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "worked-example",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Apply it to the toy logits 3, 2, 1, -1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "After "
              },
              {
                "text": "exp",
                "code": true
              },
              {
                "text": ": 20.09, 7.39, 2.72, 0.37"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Their total: 30.56"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Divide each by the total: 0.657, 0.242, 0.089, 0.012"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The four results are all between 0 and 1 and add up to 1. The token with the biggest logit ("
            },
            {
              "text": "mat",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ") has the biggest probability, and the order of the tokens has not changed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Two tokens whose logits differ by 1 always end up with probabilities in the ratio e, about 2.7 to 1. Here 0.657 is about 2.7 times 0.242."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 14)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
